<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xml:id="sec-solutions4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Molarity</title>

    <p>
        Chemists often use a different unit of concentration because it's often
        helpful to know the number of moles of solute dissolved in solution
        (because knowing the number of moles is critical if we're doing any
        calculations involving chemical reactions).
        This unit of concentration is called <term><xref ref="def-molarity" text="custom">molarity</xref></term>.
        Molarity has a symbol of capital M, which is a shorthand abbreviation
        for moles/L.  When reading concentrations that use molarity, the symbol M is typically read as 'molar'; so a 3.0 M solution of sodium chloride is often said to be a 3.0 molar solution of sodium chloride.
        Thus a 3.0 M solution of sodium chloride has 3.0 moles of sodium
        chloride dissolved in 1 L of solution.
        This can be expressed using the following formula: </p>
        <p><me>Molarity\ =\ \frac{moles\ of\ solute}{L\ of\ solution}</me></p>

            <figure>
                <caption>A dad joke to increase your 'concentration'...</caption>
                <image source="chemistry_dad_joke_molarity.svg">
                    <shortdescription>A chemistry teacher telling his embarrassed students a dad joke: Why should chemistry students always tell the truth? Because they have high 'molarity' standards.</shortdescription>
                </image>
                <p>
                    This image and joke were made with the help of ChatGPT.
                </p>
            </figure>
        <p>Thus, if 1.5 moles of solute is
        dissolved in 0.50 L of solution, the molarity can be calculated as follows:</p>
        <p><me>Molarity=\frac{1.5\ moles\ solute}{0.50\ L\ solution}\ =\ 3.0\ M</me></p>
        <p>    
        We can also append the metric prefixes on molarity (ex.
        mM; read as millimolar).
        The prefix pertains to the number of moles of solute.
        So mM means <me>\frac{millimoles\ of\ solute}{L\ of\ solution}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
        Many times in chemistry, molarity must be used as a conversion factor.  If we want to convert between liters of solution and moles of solute, molarity is the perfect conversion factor to use.  To do this, we simply take the molarity and write it as that many moles of solute in 1 L of solution.  See the example below:
        <me>4.5\ M\ NaCl\ \rightarrow\ \frac{4.5\ moles\ NaCl}{1\ L\ solution}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
        See the example problem below where molarity is used as a conversion factor
    </p>
    <assemblage>
        <title>Using molarity as a conversion factor</title>
        <p>
            <term>Problem:</term> How many grams of sodium hydroxide are contained in a 150 mL of a 500.0
        mM solution of sodium hydroxide.
        </p>
        <p>
            <term>Solution:</term>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Convert volume and molarity to their base units.
        Volume should be in liters and we want to remove prefixes from molarity
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <me>150\ mL\times\frac{10^{-3}\ L}{1\ mL}=0.15\ L</me>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <me>500.0\ mM\times\frac{10^{-3}\ M}{1\ mM}=0.5000\ M</me>
                                </p>
                            </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
            <li>
                <p>Write molarity as a conversion factor</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <me>0.5000\ M\ =\ \frac{0.5000\ moles\ sodium\ hydroxide}{1\ L\ solution}</me>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Do dimensional analysis:
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <me>0.15\ L\ solution\ \times\ \frac{0.5000\ moles\ sodium\ hydroxide}{1\ L\ solution}\</me><me> =\ 0.075\ moles\ sodium\ hydroxide</me>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Once we know the number of moles, we can calculate the number of grams using the molar mass of sodium hydroxide (NaOH)
                            </p>
                            
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        <me>0.075\ moles\ sodium\ hydroxide\ \times\ \frac{40.00\ g\ sodium\ hydroxide}{1\ mole\ sodium\ hydroxide}\</me><me> =\ 3.0\ g\ sodium\ hydroxide</me>
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                    </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercises>
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercisegroup>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Calculate the molarity of the following solutions:
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        0.15 moles of NaCl dissolved in 0.37 L of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        0.41M
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        0.070 moles of NaCl dissolved in 175 mL of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        0.40 M
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        17 g of NaCl dissolved in 570 mL of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        0.51 M
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many moles of sodium chloride are needed to prepare 45
                    mL of a 3.0M solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    0.14 moles
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many grams of sodium chloride are needed to prepare 45
                    mL of a 3.0 M solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    7.9g
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many mg of sodium chloride are contained in 1.00 mL of a
                    175 mM solution of sodium chloride?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    10.2 mg
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>